                      HISTORY OF WALL STREET BLUES

V1.0 (27 September 1994) Primary game constructed. This version was not 
     released.

v1.1 (1 October 1994) Bugs removed from arithmatic on company stock pricing. 

     All additions to stocks made to even numbers to prevent breakdown into 
     several decimal places for stock prices.

     Stocks that split had odd numbers when the stock price was ending with
     2 or 0. Stocks now split to $108.

     CNet mail reminder added to remind players to invest thier dividends.

     Time award added to promote game playing/testing.

V1.2 (4 October 1994) Error routines added to ease installation.
     
     MonthlyMaint file name changed to "Reset". 

     Main program file name changed to "WallStreet".

     Added Sort file execution to the end of Maint.

V1.3 (27 October 1994) Fixed routine that checks for a stock price being $0
     and not allowing anyone to purchase that stock. I saw this during
     playtesting and put in the fix however some variables were not set
     right.
     
     Added additional spaces for those investors who go above 1,000,000.
     They must have learned of the bug that would allow buying a stock
     priced at $0!

V2.0 (5 November 1994) Added Dow Jones index and percent change from
     previous day earnings.
     
     Added Date file that checks to see if maintenance has already run yet
     for that day. This file is now put in BBS events INSTEAD of Maint.
     Everthing is linked together under it.

     (12 November 1994) Added company history files. Company Biographies
     changed to Company Prospectus and shows the percentage gained per year
     as well as previous years prices. Also shows the average percent
     gained from each stock. Some percentages have been taken out of the
     average to give a more accurate percentage. -100% when the date equals
     the previous day.(Eliminates -100% when a certain day does not have a
     price above $0 yet.) Stock split percentages. (Would give false
     percents when a stock was $196 and then it showed $108 the next day.)
     Any percentage given when the previous days stock price equals $0.
     I've decided not to have the Prospectus reset every month so users
     would have something to go by the following month.

     Added additional main menu items so the games initial screens could be
     accessed from the main menu. I always wanted to see them again!

     Started requesting donations for this pfile. Send in yours today! See
     the DOCS file to find out where to send them.

     (4 December 1994) Added the A command to buying stocks. Makes it
     easier to buy the maximum amounts! Suggested by Mike Miller, thanks!

     Joined the sell and buy screens into one. Not as much switching back
     and forth! Suggested by Mike Miller, thanks!
   
     Changed the Date file to run all events. Couldn't get the reset file
     to run on the first of every month like CNet says it should. The date
     file will now recognize the first of every month and run reset. The
     date file was helpful in ensuring everything in maintenance ran in the
     right order.

     Corrected bugs found in the prospectus file. All values equaling -100%
     are no longer included in the average gain percentage. It would be an
     excessive amount of work, for what it's worth, to read these values 
     in correctly.

     Hey, learned more about Arexx programming! Never can stop learning!

v2.1 (16 January 1995) Well, I sent out v2.0 without a date.data file and 
     it seems I forgot to put a check to see if it was there so it could be 
     made. That has been fixed.

     I had a user that went for broke and ended up that way. Miller Mining
     can do that to you. So with his $3.04 he started buying fractions of a
     share. Well, it worked, except it messed up the rest of the game. Then
     I realized that from the menu options screen you could also select a
     fraction there. Another bad flaw. Really messes up the game. You
     really wouldn't want a user acting up like that because he is sore about
     losing his gamble. I had to create a routine before to make the A=Max
     function work properly. I didn't want it to return anything to the
     right of the decimal point. The same is so with the buying of shares
     and the menu items. The routine is called CUTIT. Since I accessed it
     once for the A=Max function, I incorporated it into the GETNUM
     function also. It works well now. Also added a line that exits the
     game should anyone enter a number lower than 1.

     I took a close look at the CUTIT function and realized it was faulty.
     Seems I didn't set it up right for the higher numbers. That has been 
     fixed also.

v2.2 (29 March 1995) Fixed the ADDTIME command in TimeAward. I had this set
     to 30 and the time is actually measured in 10ths. The old time had 30,
     the new time has been changed to 300. Bug credits to Ray Morrell of
     Planet X Productions.

v2.3 (19 July 1996) The game now has correct stock splits. Before I could
     not figure out how to get a split without having a .5 or half a share
     for a result. The result was that all shares were split to 108. This 
     may confuse the data tables for the stocks since it relied on
     eliminating data from the dates 108 was posted (an intentional 50%
     drop). So that data may become seriously off for awhile. (Sorry no
     time to mess with it, I'm off for Bosnia in a week.)

     Ok, I find it hard to believe that anyone could get to 1,000,000 in
     just one month, probably because I've never done it. Seems that the
     game likes to cheat the millionaires on the high score screens. I kept 
     hearing of this bug and couldn't believe it was that big of a problem.
     It is now fixed. However, the same problem will occur if you achieve
     100,000,000.

     I've added a new feature in the maintenance routine. Every player is
     now paid $5000 per day. I added this to give every one more to do on a
     daily basis. Maybe to help them reach the 100,000,000 error level...
     The $5000 represents an annual pay that would support $5000 a year
     investing. It would be nice to make that kind of money! Remember that
     everyday in Wall Street Blues represents a full year.

v3.0 (30 July 96) Added the all-time highest scorer record. This gives the
     players something to shoot for when they are winning big! Also cleaned
     up some of the printing in the maintenance routines.

     (2 August 96) Added a new screen to show the previous day's high and
     low gainers along side the Dow Jones percentage. Also added this to the
     portfolio screen for every player.

     (21 August 96) Added the dow jones all-time high. This will let you know
     if a particular month is better than the last. Thinking about adding a
     daily all-time high for this. Some months are better than others.
   
     Combined the portfolio and buy screens. They were so similar that it
     only made sense to do it. 

     Added the yesterdays percent gain to the buy screen for each user. 
     Thinking about adding an average gain amount for the month to this 
     screen. There is still a little more room for info on this screen.

     Added the fix to the prospectus routines to make the averages correct.
     Good thing I brought the Amiga to Bosnia with me. Also added a blank
     percentage to the current day. Averages are computed for the days that 
     have passed in the month, not from the days in the previous month.

     Found and corrected a nasty little bug that would put Allison Oil
     bankrupt over night no matter what the current price per share was. Strange
     I had looked for this bug before and couldn't find it.

     Added the interesting facts screen. The game will now track who has the
     highest score by a given date as well as the dow jones high.
 
     Added the shares sold for each company to the interesting facts screen.
     Also added this to the company's prospectus screen.

     Wall Street Blues v3.0 was created from my spare time while serving with
     the Army in Bosnia/Herzogovina in support of Operation Joint Endeavor.
     Comments on the game or suggested improvements can be sent by way of e-mail
     to me. If they are sent before December 96 I'll recieve them while I'm
     still in Bosnia. Raves and reviews are most appreciated!

v3.1 (1 Oct 96) I released version 3.0 too soon! I found a bug that kept the 
     same High Scorer Name only with the new highest amount. A simple
     variable change fixed that.

     Fixed another bug that had incorrect data for day 01.

     Changed the load routines to occur during the display of the title
     screen. This change cuts down on player waiting time.

     I've noticed another bug in the buy routines. If you have lots of
     money and you want to buy a stock priced at $5 a share, there is a
     limit to how many you can buy at one time. You can eventually buy as
     much as you want but there seems to be a limit on the A or all
     command. I'm not sure why this is so if any one knows pass it along to
     me!

     (15 Oct 96) Got word of a bug in the installation process of the game. 
     I always get caught up when I don't test the installation process
     thoroughly. Anyway, the ALL file was not being made and maintenance
     would look for it. When maintenance didn't find it it would quit and
     never reach the updating part of maint. Anyway, I got it fixed so it
     creates it right away now if it doesn't exist. This allows maintenance
     to complete and set up correctly. I also added maintenance to the
     reset feature to be sure everything ran well. Thanks to the sysop who
     reported this bug. Version 3.1 is on the way!

     (17 Oct 96) How could I have missed this one? The Dow Jones is
     configured to have one of each stock so it can produce an average gain
     or loss for all the stocks evenly. When a stock went broke then it
     would reset to just 1 share of that stock and start all over. If a
     stock split then this number should have doubled like the number of
     shares doubles for each individual. Instead of doubling I had it set
     up to only add one share when the stock split. Big error of course.
     This has been fixed.

     Had a bug where a new member who chose not to play would always lose
     -100% for not investing. This has been changed to reflect 0% when
     there is no change. Now version 3.1 should be ready to release,
     provided our LAN feed is up tommorrow. It was down all day and it's my
     only way to send things out of Bosnia.

     (26 Dec 96) Another bug was found. I can't believe it didn't come up
     during testing. When the player who is winning logs on the first time
     of the day the winning file is checked to see if it's him. It then
     awards the free time and deletes the file to prevent free time from
     being added everytime he calls. During the updates I made in Bosnia, I
     made the winning file neccessary in the main game file. So when this
     file gets deleted the game crashes until maintenance makes another
     file and then it gets deleted... This doesn't affect the SysOps who
     don't use this feature in the welcome file. The fix here so far is to
     delete the delete command in the file Time Award. An idea would be to
     have this file copied at maint to ram and delete it from there.

     Found the reason why there was a maximum limit to how many shares you
     could buy with the A command. At the end of the program is the
     function called CUTIT. It cuts the numbers down so that there are no 
     decimal places. This creates whole numbers. The first one limits the
     number to 1 charachter, the next to 2 charachters, and the next to 3
     charachters. The rest are also limited to three charachters in error.
     They should increment by 1 all the way down.

v3.2 (3 Apr 97) In March of 1995 a suggestion was made to change the random 
     seed generator to get a true random number. This suggestion was given by
     Ray Morrell, aka The Editor. Although I've taken this up with future
     programs (Pugilistic Glory) I failed to add it to the maint file for
     Wall Street Blues. In the latest version 3.1a the oil company always
     leads to winning. This may be caused by the random seed generator not
     working properly. Testing here on my computer may well prove this.
     Sorry Ray, I did get your message and fully planned to use this
     advice. We'll see how this version works before I send it out.

     Thomas Sherman wrote to inform me that when selecting the amount of
     shares to buy, if any other letter was entered besides an 'A' then the
     program would error. Yes, a part of the code which checks for the
     proper imput was not working correctly. This has been changed. The 'A'
     command is still accepted however, if any other letter is entered the
     user will be prompted for a number. Simply hitting return at this
     prompt will default to '0'.

v4.0 Of course with the updating to v4.0 a change to ANSI screens seemed
     appropriate. It lends a new look to the game. Some of these screens 
     are difficult to change as they worked well with a black backround.
     You can probably tell I'm not good with ANSI screens or mixing colors.
     I had my wife help me with the colors matching. Yea, she buys my
     clothes too!

     Of course the winning file could not be deleted as the main program
     asked for this file to be present. Now, when the winning file is
     created during maintenance a winningaw file is created also. It's
     exactly the same as the winning file. The Timeaward will now look for
     and delete the winningaw file instead.

     Updated the user instructions file.

     Added stock percentage changes to the buy/sell screen. The room was 
     there to put something and that's what I thought of first. It gives the 
     percentage of change from the previous day. I had to fix this after
     creation due to the 100% losses it showed during stock splits. This
     was discovered and fixed in the prospectus screens and I used data
     from those files to make it accurate.

     Added last months winner to the interesting facts screen. If your game
     got screwed up and the file wasn't made correctly it will default to
     Caesar with 100000. This can be changed with a text editor on the
     LWwinner file. If you still have a good U4 file you can get last
     months winner from there.

     Added my favorite SysOp to the automobile company. Patrick Greene, my
     successor to the Gold City User's Group BBS. The Gold City User's
     Group has a bunch of die-hard Amiga users in it. All the company names
     are named after users in this group. This is no way a complete listing 
     of the user's group as it is today. These were only main participants 
     during my time. These members make up my main play testers on pfiles. 
     Especially Mike who liked to see how he could get the programs to do 
     crazy things.

     Fixed a bug that reset the DOWTOPP score every month. It now looks to
     make sure this file exists and if not then it creates one.

     (1 May 1997) Fixed bugs during monthly reset.

4.0a (18 May 1997) Was playing this thing like usual and noticed that
     Logsdon Realty took a 46% drop from the previous day. Being the
     creator of this pfile I knew this stock does not change that severely.
     Looking into the situation I noticed that the stock had split that
     day. Well, I found the error in the DowMaint file. The user I had
     invested in that stock seems to have had his stock total doubled every
     split so that wasn't a problem. What was affected was the prospectus,
     (who looks at this?) the newest addition to the Buy/Sell screen
     showing percentages, and of course the dow jones average/top score.

4.1  (1 July 1997) Added byte credit awards to the monthly winner. The
     value of these credits can be adjusted in the timeaward file to suit
     your bbs needs. Of course this could be a good motivator for callers
     to play if they need credits. If your BBS does not work with byte
     credits the award can be changed to another category using the correct
     long getuser command and changing the ANSI screen to reflect this. To
     remove this option remove the PZE function with a text editor and
     change line 17 in timeaward to read "then exit". Also you will need to
     use the OLDINTRO ansi in place of the INTRO file by renaming it.

     (1 August 1997) Fixed errors in game reset routine. I was waiting for
     the first of the month just to see this happen and fix it where it
     screwed up.

     (5 August 1997) Fixed an error the game had if there were no days
     listed in the days directory. The game can now start with no daily
     records and continue writing them as the game progresses.

     (28 August 1997) Buy ansi screen redone. Some displays and users would
     not show the whole screen properly. The more mode prompt was shown so
     I deleted a couple of lines in the screen to allow the screen to
     display fully in one swipe. This problem also showed up on telnet
     BBSs. Hopefully this will fix that as well.

     This version is released as a final version for Wall Street Blues.
     I've spent many hours in programming this thing. It was my first pfile
     for CNet and a learning experience as well. I have strived for
     accuracy in all the numbers that are displayed in this game and with
     some of the options that are included, this became difficult. If you
     see numbers that "just can't be right", consider that all users are
     collecting $5000 per day and this affects those numbers.

     Bug fixes will still be addressed, so if you find them, send them my
     way. Over the years of developing this pfile I have recieved two
     donations from SysOps appreciating my work. Travis ? and Thomas
     Sherman. I didn't really expect alot of donations since it is not
     crippled in any way. People don't pay for something that works
     already. Only cure is to move on to C programming.
